WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:06.680
Password security depends on careful handling, strong storage, safe reset flows, monitoring, and operational readiness.

00:00:06.900 --> 00:00:14.180
Passwords are still widely used, and attackers continue to exploit phishing, reuse, breach replay, and credential stuffing.

00:00:14.400 --> 00:00:22.320
Developers should assume that some credentials will eventually be guessed, stolen, reused, leaked, or exposed, then design systems to limit the damage.

00:00:22.540 --> 00:00:32.220
Strong credential storage uses purpose-built password hashing, unique salts, appropriate cost settings, protected secrets, access control, and migration planning.

00:00:32.440 --> 00:00:39.640
Algorithms such as Argon2, bcrypt, scrypt, and PBKDF2 should be used through established libraries.

00:00:39.860 --> 00:00:43.540
Fast general-purpose hashes and custom schemes should be avoided.

00:00:43.760 --> 00:00:51.920
Stored password hashes remain sensitive and should be protected in databases, backups, logs, exports, and support workflows.

00:00:52.140 --> 00:00:55.100
Good authentication flows add layers around storage.

00:00:55.320 --> 00:01:06.360
Login rate limits, credential stuffing defenses, MFA, safe reset tokens, careful error messages, suspicious login monitoring, and session invalidation after password changes all reduce risk.

00:01:06.580 --> 00:01:15.060
These controls should be tested against real abuse cases and balanced against user experience so that security does not create unnecessary lockout or recovery problems.

00:01:15.280 --> 00:01:17.280
The final goal is resilience.

00:01:17.500 --> 00:01:29.340
When credentials are exposed, the organization should know how to assess impact, rotate secrets, force resets when needed, notify users, upgrade hash parameters, and monitor for abuse.

00:01:29.560 --> 00:01:33.000
Password security is not a one-time implementation detail.

00:01:33.220 --> 00:01:39.780
It is a lifecycle that spans design, code, operations, incident response, and continuous review.
